-Linear Style Transfer (LST) is a method that improves efficiency over AdaIN by learning linear transformations directly to the feature maps, realizing fast and scalable style transfer across multiple styles [S1].
+Linear Style Transfer (LST) is a method that improves efficiency over AdaIN by learning linear transformations directly to the feature maps, realizing fast and scalable style transfer across multiple styles [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** AdaIN is computationally efficient compared to iterative optimization-based methods like NST because it is a feedforward operation without backpropagation or gradient descent steps [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing feature maps from the content and style images, typically from intermediate layers of a CNN like VGG-19 [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Enables arbitrary style transfer without per-image optimization; fast inference; preserves content structure better than pixel-level color transfer [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the feature extractor; may not preserve fine-grained structural details as well as segmentation-based methods [S1].
-- **When to use:** When fast, arbitrary style transfer is needed without per-scene optimization.
-- **When not to use:** When photorealistic structure preservation is critical and segmentation guidance is required (use FPRF or similar) [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- AdaIN is computationally efficient as it involves only element-wise operations and statistics computation, avoiding iterative optimization [S2].
+- In volume rendering, the additional MLP for TF descriptor modulation adds minimal overhead compared to the decoder itself [S1].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+- Specific performance metrics are not provided in the sources. Training for the volume rendering application took around 20 hours on a single RTX 2080 GPU [S1].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- Strengths: Enables arbitrary style transfer without per-image optimization; preserves content structure effectively [S2].
+- Limitations: May struggle with extreme style-content mismatches; requires careful integration into decoder architectures for volume rendering [S1].
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when arbitrary style transfer is required in a feed-forward manner, particularly in neural rendering pipelines where explicit transfer function modulation is needed [S1, S2].
+- Avoid when photorealistic structure preservation is critical without artistic stylization, in which case FPRF or segmentation-guided methods may be more appropriate [S1].
 
 ## Background
-AdaIN was introduced to address the computational expense and slowness of earlier neural style transfer methods that relied on iterative optimization [S1]. It utilizes VGG-19 as a feature extractor [S1]. The method was developed to enable arbitrary style transfer by directly aligning the mean and variance of feature maps [S1]. It has been integrated into various 3D editing and generation pipelines, such as those involving NeRF and 3D Gaussian Splatting, for efficient style manipulation [S1].
+AdaIN was introduced as a method to achieve arbitrary style transfer by aligning feature map statistics [S2]. It has been adapted for use in explicit volume rendering to modulate feature tensors in the decoder using transfer function descriptors, replacing instance normalization layers with AdaIN layers [S1]. The technique builds on the concept of Instance Normalization, which normalizes tensors to zero mean and unit variance [S1].
 
